      "topic_id": "algebraic_proof",
      "prompt": "Prove algebraically that n^2 - n is always even.",
      "solution_steps": [
        "n^2 - n factorises as n(n - 1), the product of two consecutive integers n - 1 and n.",
        "Write the given expression algebraically: n^2 - n.",
        "Expand and simplify: n^2 - n = n(n - 1).",
        "Factorise/rearrange to expose the structure: n(n - 1) = if n is even, n(n - 1) is even × anything = even; if n is odd, then n - 1 is even, so n(n - 1) is anything × even = even.",
        "Either n or n - 1 must be even (they are consecutive integers, so they can't both be odd), so the product always has an even factor - n^2 - n is always even."
      ],
      "final_answer": "Proven: n^2 - n = n(n - 1) is always even, since one of n, n - 1 is always even.",
